// Practice quiz: Unit tests with JUnit, written test-first
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.
// GIFT forbids the angle-bracket characters, so lambdas and comparisons are written in words.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/testing-and-java-review/junit-test-first

::o1 why watch it fail::In test-first work, why run a new test before writing the code it checks? {
=To see it fail, which proves the test can catch the missing behavior#Correct. A red run shows the test really checks something; a test that could never fail proves nothing.
~Because JUnit refuses to run a test that has never failed#Not quite. JUnit runs any test method; watching it fail is a habit, not a JUnit rule.
~To let JUnit record the expected value for later runs#Not quite. You write the expected value yourself in the assertion.
~To make the production class compile faster#Not quite. Running a test does not change how fast code compiles.
}

::o1 runnable test method::Which method can JUnit run as a test? {
=A method marked @Test, declared void addsTax(), with no return value#Correct. A test method is marked @Test, returns void and is not private; public is optional.
~A method marked @Test, declared int addsTax(), that returns the total#Not quite. A test method must not return a value.
~A method marked @Test, declared private void addsTax()#Not quite. A test method must not be private.
~A method declared void addsTax() with no annotation#Not quite. Without @Test, JUnit does not treat it as a test.
}

::o2 swapped arguments::A test writes assertEquals(total, 120), where total is the value the method returned. The method returns 100. What happens? {
=The test fails, but the message wrongly reports 100 as expected and 120 as actual#Correct. assertEquals takes the expected value first; swapping them still fails the test but makes the message misleading.
~The test passes, because both arguments are ints#Not quite. assertEquals compares the values, and 100 and 120 differ.
~The code does not compile#Not quite. Both arguments are ints, so the call compiles; the order is a convention JUnit cannot check.
~JUnit notices the swap and reports 120 as expected#Not quite. JUnit trusts the argument order you wrote.
}

::o2 the act line::A test for Cart.addItem has three parts. Which line is the act part? {
=cart.addItem("pen", 2);#Correct. Act is the one call to the behavior being tested.
~Cart cart \= new Cart();#Not quite. Creating the object is the arrange part.
~assertEquals(1, cart.size());#Not quite. Comparing the result with the expected value is the assert part.
~@Test#Not quite. The annotation marks the method as a test; it is not one of the three parts.
}

::o3 why the lambda::Why is the call that should be refused written inside a lambda passed to assertThrows, not on the line before it? {
=So assertThrows makes the call itself and can catch the exception#Correct. A bare call on the line before would throw first and stop the test with an error.
~Because a lambda makes the call run faster#Not quite. The lambda decides who makes the call, not how fast it runs.
~Because JUnit skips any line that comes before an assertion#Not quite. JUnit runs every line; that is exactly why the bare call would throw too early.
~Because every JUnit assertion needs a lambda#Not quite. assertEquals and assertTrue take plain values.
}

::o3 a subtype is thrown::A test expects IllegalArgumentException.class, and its lambda calls a parse method that throws NumberFormatException, a subclass of IllegalArgumentException. What happens? {
=The test passes, because a subtype of the expected type counts#Correct. assertThrows passes for the expected exception type or any subtype, and returns the exception.
~The test fails, because the type must match exactly#Not quite. That stricter check is a different method; assertThrows accepts subtypes.
~The test fails, because assertThrows also needs the message#Not quite. You may check the message with the returned exception, but assertThrows does not require it.
~The test passes even if parse throws nothing#Not quite. assertThrows fails when nothing is thrown.
}
